§ `s 3 -- a Western Australian death, and a reportable one`

-- c086: Graham died at home in WA.
#ASSERT `Is this a Western Australian death` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify`
-- c086: expected, natural, certified by his GP -- not reportable, as the scenario says.
#ASSERT NOT `Is this a reportable death` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify`
-- Limb (i): a death in WA with no s 44 certificate is reportable, however natural.
#ASSERT `Is this a reportable death` `c086 variant: no doctor will certify the cause`
-- Limb (a): unexpected is enough, even with a certificate.
#ASSERT `Is this a reportable death` `c086 variant: sudden and unexpected, the GP certifies`
-- Limb (e): held in care immediately before death.
#ASSERT `Is this a reportable death` `c086 variant: dies in a police lock-up`
-- s 3A: a voluntary assisted death is not reportable, though no s 44 certificate is given.
#ASSERT NOT `Is this a reportable death` `c086 variant: voluntary assisted dying`
-- A WA death (body here, ordinarily resident) but limb (j) is not met: certified where he died.
#ASSERT NOT `Is this a reportable death` `c086 variant: dies on holiday in Bali, certified there, body flown home`

§ `s 17 -- must I report it, to whom, and the penalty`

-- c086: Chloe need not report Graham's death.
#ASSERT NOT `Must I report this death` `Chloe, Graham's daughter` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify`
-- c086: nor need his GP; he certifies instead.
#ASSERT NOT `Must I report this death` `Graham's GP, called to the house` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify`
#ASSERT `Must I report this death` `Chloe, Graham's daughter` `c086 variant: a fall at home`
-- s 17(1): reasonable grounds to believe it has already been reported.
#ASSERT NOT `Must I report this death` `Chloe, knowing the police have already been told` `c086 variant: a fall at home`
-- s 17(4).
#ASSERT NOT `Must I report this death` `a second doctor present, after the first reported to the coroner` `c086 variant: a fall at home`
-- s 17(5).
#ASSERT `Must I report this death` `the officer in charge of the lock-up` `c086 variant: dies in a police lock-up`
#ASSERT `Whom must I report to` `Chloe, Graham's daughter` `c086 variant: a fall at home` EQUALS `a coroner or a police officer, immediately (s 17(1))`
-- A doctor must report to a coroner, not the police.
#ASSERT `Whom must I report to` `Graham's GP, called to the house` `c086 variant: a fall at home` EQUALS `a coroner, immediately (s 17(3) or (5))`
-- c086
#ASSERT `Whom must I report to` `Chloe, Graham's daughter` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify` EQUALS `no one: I need not report this death`
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be fined for not reporting` `Chloe, Graham's daughter` `c086 variant: a fall at home` EQUALS 1000
-- s 17(4): relieved of reporting, not of helping.
#ASSERT `Must I give the coroner information` `a second doctor present, after the first reported to the coroner` FALSE

§ `ss 19, 19A -- will the coroner investigate`

-- c086
#ASSERT `Will the coroner investigate` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify` `no directions` FALSE EQUALS `no: it is not a reportable death, so the coroner has no jurisdiction (s 19(1))`
#ASSERT `Will the coroner investigate` `c086 variant: sudden and unexpected, the GP certifies` `no directions` TRUE EQUALS `the coroner need not investigate: natural causes, reportable only because unexpected (s 19A)`
-- No s 44 certificate makes limb (i) apply too, so the death is not reportable 'solely' because unexpected.
#ASSERT `Will the coroner investigate` `c086 variant: sudden and unexpected, no doctor will certify` `no directions` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the death must be investigated (ss 8(c), 19(1))`
-- s 19A(3)(b): never for a death during an anaesthetic.
#ASSERT `Will the coroner investigate` `c086 variant: dies during an anaesthetic, unexpectedly, certified` `no directions` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the death must be investigated (ss 8(c), 19(1))`

§ `ss 22-24 -- will there be an inquest, and may we ask for one`

-- c086
#ASSERT `Must an inquest be held` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify` `no directions` EQUALS `no: the coroner has no jurisdiction, so there can be no inquest`
#ASSERT `Must an inquest be held` `c086 variant: dies in a police lock-up` `no directions` EQUALS `yes: an inquest must be held (s 22(1) or s 23(2))`
#ASSERT `Must an inquest be held` `c086 variant: a fall at home` `no directions` EQUALS `only if the coroner thinks it desirable (s 22(2)); we may ask for one (s 24)`
#ASSERT `Must an inquest be held` `c086 variant: a fall at home` `the State Coroner has directed an inquest` EQUALS `yes: an inquest must be held (s 22(1) or s 23(2))`
#ASSERT `May I ask for an inquest` `c086 variant: a fall at home` TRUE
-- Refused; the seventh day after notice.
#ASSERT `May I apply to the Supreme Court for an inquest` TRUE 7 TRUE 0
#ASSERT NOT `May I apply to the Supreme Court for an inquest` TRUE 8 TRUE 0
-- No reply within 3 months.
#ASSERT `May I apply to the Supreme Court for an inquest` FALSE 0 FALSE 3

§ `s 37(5) -- the senior next of kin`

-- c086: Sue, living with Graham, is first.
#ASSERT `What is my place in the order of next of kin` `Sue, Graham's wife, living with him` EQUALS 1
-- Chloe takes (c), not (f): the higher place.
#ASSERT `What is my place in the order of next of kin` `Chloe, Graham's adult daughter and executor` EQUALS 3
-- c086
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `Sue, Graham's wife, living with him` 3 EQUALS `yes: I am the senior next of kin`
-- c086: while Sue is available, Chloe is not.
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `Chloe, Graham's adult daughter and executor` 1 EQUALS `no: someone available ranks ahead of me, or I am not on the list`
-- After Sue: Chloe and an adult brother.
#ASSERT `Am I the senior next of kin` `Chloe, Graham's adult daughter and executor` 3 EQUALS `I share the first place with another available person; s 37(5) does not say who goes first`

§ `ss 20, 25(4), 30(2) -- what we must be told and allowed`

-- Any next of kin on the s 37(5) list, not only the senior.
#ASSERT `Must the coroner give me the section 20 information` `Chloe, Graham's adult daughter and executor` `c086 variant: a fall at home`
#ASSERT `Must the coroner give me written information on the post mortem` `Sue, Graham's wife, living with him` 22 FALSE FALSE

§ `s 37 -- objecting to a post mortem`

#ASSERT NOT `Can the post mortem go ahead despite my objection` 1 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- After 2 clear working days, even if an application to the Supreme Court is pending.
#ASSERT `Can the post mortem go ahead despite my objection` 2 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- The coroner believes it must be done immediately.
#ASSERT `Can the post mortem go ahead despite my objection` 0 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE

§ `ss 29, 30 -- when the body is released`

-- c086: the funeral director may take Graham's body.
#ASSERT `Does the coroner hold the body` `c086: Graham at 82, at home, expected, natural, his GP will certify` FALSE EQUALS `not held by the coroner: the Coroners Act does not hold it back`
#ASSERT `Does the coroner hold the body` `c086 variant: a fall at home` FALSE EQUALS `held by the coroner until the s 29 certificate is issued (s 30(1))`
-- This Act permits cremation on the coroner's certificate; the Cremation Act 1929 s 8A(a) still asks for a s 44 certificate.
#ASSERT `Does the coroner hold the body` `c086 variant: no doctor will certify the cause` TRUE EQUALS `released: the s 29 certificate permits burial, cremation or other disposal`
#ASSERT `When can the disposal certificate issue` `a request was refused and the 2 clear working days to apply to the Supreme Court are running` EQUALS `not yet: the s 36 application or the time for it must run out first (s 29(2))`
#ASSERT `When can the disposal certificate issue` `the Supreme Court has ordered a post mortem (s 36(4))` EQUALS `only the State Coroner may issue it, after the ordered post mortem (ss 29(3), 36(4))`
